== Short description ==
historian2grafana gives site engineers a browser-based view of production measurements recorded in AVEVA Historian. It reads selected process tags from one or more archives, keeps a local history in PostgreSQL and presents trends and calculated indicators in the application and Grafana. For example, an engineer can select a shift, plot box weight against its target and display the cumulative weight deviation in kilograms. The application reads from the production archive. It sends no commands to production equipment.

== Requirements ==
=== Functional Requirements ===
{| class="wikitable"
! Function !! Requestor's response
|-
| Connect to data sources || Configure one or more AVEVA Historian SQL Server connections. For each source, test login, tag discovery and history reads. Select tags with include/exclude patterns. Each source has its own schedule and progress.
|-
| Collect production history || Load a configurable initial period (24 hours by default), then poll for new records (5 seconds by default). Store the tag, timestamp, value and quality. Resume from saved copy progress after an interruption and reread an overlapping interval to pick up late changes without duplicating matching records.
|-
| Inspect and export data || Compare multiple tags over a selected period, use separate scales for different engineering units and export the selected series to CSV.
|-
| Calculate indicators || Run saved scripts on the local data to identify peaks and threshold excursions, aggregate by day, week or month, and calculate running totals. For box weight, calculate measured weight minus target for each weighing. Positive deviations give overweight, the magnitude of negative deviations gives underweight, and their signed sum gives net deviation. Counting boxes requires a validated one-to-one relationship between archive events and weighings.
|-
| Publish results || Display a calculated series as a trend or a single result as a numeric card. Save the selected panels as a Grafana dashboard. Users can select tags and scripts in Grafana without writing SQL.
|}

=== Non-Functional Requirements ===
{| class="wikitable"
! Requirement name !! Requestor's response
|-
| Data classification || Scope includes equipment and process tag names, values, timestamps, quality, production trends, calculated deviations and exported reports. Data owner to assign the Mars classification for the approved tag set and derived reports. Connection credentials require separate protection.
|-
| Personal data || The weighing use case requires equipment and measurement data only. String tags, tag descriptions, configured Windows/SQL login names, Grafana accounts and host/access logs may contain personal identifiers. Confirm selected fields, whether accounts identify employees or contractors, and the number of individuals in each applicable category. Record zero only for a verified category.
|-
| Availability || An outage delays analytics and dashboards. Historian acquisition operates independently. Service window [hours/month], acceptable unplanned downtime [hours/month] and planned maintenance [hours/month] require site-owner approval. Monthly availability (%) = 100 × (1 − unplanned downtime / (service hours − planned downtime)), using hours within the agreed service window.
|-
| Disaster recovery – Restoration Time Objective (RTO) || [Hours to be agreed with the site owner.] Measure from disaster declaration until the application and Grafana are available on a replacement host, the collector is reading, and a reference panel has been verified. Record outstanding historical catch-up separately.
|-
| Disaster recovery – Restoration Point Objective (RPO) || [Maximum acceptable loss in hours to be agreed.] Cover local settings, scripts, dashboards, Grafana state and measurements. Older measurements can be reloaded only while retained and readable in the source archive. The local configuration and saved analyses depend on a recoverable backup.
|-
| Recovery point objective in case of unplanned downtime || For an interruption with the local database intact, the collector resumes from its saved progress. Recovery of the complete interval depends on the source retaining those records. Agree the maximum acceptable unrecoverable gap [minutes/hours] and verify by interrupting collection and comparing the recovered interval with Historian.
|-
| Total number of users || Intended users are site engineers analysing process data and personnel authorised to configure collection and dashboards. Confirm [named users], [peak concurrent viewers], [maintainers] and [peak usage hours] with the site.
|-
| Total number of transactions/requests per time period || At the default 5-second interval, one continuously running source schedules approximately 17,280 polling cycles per 24 hours. A cycle can issue several SQL queries and return many measurements. Record actual cycles/day, rows/day, dashboard requests/day and peak concurrent viewers during a representative shift.
|-
| Performance || For pilot acceptance, measure time from an archived timestamp to the first visible point on a live panel. A delay of 10–15 seconds was identified as tolerable when discussing the prospective second archive. Confirm the target for the current AVEVA deployment. Measure raw trends and calculated Stat panels separately. Also time opening a 24-hour trend and calculating a shift total, recording tag count, rows, concurrent viewers and 95th-percentile time. Site results and acceptance limits are pending.
|}

=== Support Requirements ===
{| class="wikitable"
! Requirement name !! Requestor's response
|-
| Planned maintenance || Proposed for site approval. At the agreed interval, check disk growth, backup completion and readability. In a scheduled window, back up the installation, stop collection and services, apply the tested update, restart and verify one source, one trend and one calculated panel. Collection and views may be interrupted during the update. Confirm maintenance owner, frequency and permitted downtime.
|-
| Condition based maintenance || If a source stops advancing, check its last successful cycle, network route, credentials and archive access. After recovery, verify catch-up for the affected interval. If disk space approaches the agreed minimum, check table and index sizes and arrange capacity or approved retention action. If the application responds but a panel is stale, compare the last source timestamp, local copy progress and Grafana refresh time. Site to assign alert thresholds, recipients and escalation contacts.
|}

