== System Design ==
{| class="wikitable"
! Category !! Solution
|-
| Hosting model || The reviewed configuration is the Windows offline package without Docker. The application, PostgreSQL and Grafana run on one Windows host. Confirm the production host, site/network segment, physical or virtual hosting and operational owner.
|-
| Non-prod environments || The repository provides automated tests and a Historian emulator. A dedicated site test environment, representative tag set and release acceptance process require confirmation.
|-
| Operating system || The offline package targets 64-bit Windows 10/11. Record the actual host edition, build and site support status.
|-
| Special Platforms || AVEVA Historian provides the source data. Grafana provides dashboards. The packaged components run locally and can operate without internet access.
|-
| Database software || Microsoft SQL Server exposes the AVEVA Historian Runtime database. PostgreSQL 16 stores the local analytical copy. The reviewed Windows package uses standard PostgreSQL without the TimescaleDB extension.
|-
| Encryption || The package serves the application and Grafana over HTTP. The Grafana PostgreSQL datasource uses a loopback connection with SSL disabled. SQL Server transport protection depends on the client driver and server configuration. The ODBC configuration allows trust of the server certificate for supported drivers. Explicit Historian passwords, when used, are stored in application settings without application-level encryption. Confirm site TLS, host and backup encryption, file permissions and credential handling.
|-
| Sizing || Allow approximately 2 GB for the Windows package, plus disk for production history, indexes, Grafana state, logs and backups. Use measured daily PostgreSQL growth × retention days, then add working space for maintenance. Record host CPU, RAM, usable disk, tag count, rows/day and measured growth during the pilot.
|-
| Application accessibility || Browser access to the application on TCP 8000 and Grafana on TCP 3000 by default. The application listens on all host interfaces. PostgreSQL listens on loopback, TCP 55432 by default. Confirm which workstations or groups may reach each web interface.
|-
| Authentication || The application's management UI and API have no built-in user sign-in. Grafana enables anonymous Viewer access and has a local administrator account in the standard package. Historian supports Windows integrated authentication or an explicit login. Confirm the approved production access mechanism for both web interfaces.
|-
| Authorization || Grafana Viewer can view dashboards. The separate Grafana database role can read the specified telemetry relations. The application API has no per-user role checks for changing sources, starting/stopping collection, editing scripts or saving/deleting dashboards. Grafana Viewer permissions do not govern these API operations. Confirm how management access will be restricted at the site.
|-
| Integrations || The collector initiates read-only queries to AVEVA Historian over SQL Server/TDS (normally TCP 1433 or the configured instance port). It writes to local PostgreSQL. Grafana queries the application through its Graphite-compatible HTTP API or reads telemetry directly from PostgreSQL. The application saves dashboard definitions as JSON files loaded by Grafana. FactoryTalk Historian SE via PI Data Archive is a prospective integration. TCP 5450 reachability was checked from the application host. Connector implementation, licensing and successful tag reads are outstanding.
|-
| Application logging || Per-source events and recent polling cycles are visible on the Copying page. Runtime messages go to the launcher console. PostgreSQL and Grafana write local logs, including data\postgres.log and data\grafana.log. Confirm log retention and any central collection.
|-
| Application monitoring || /healthz checks that the web process responds. Use the Copying page or /api/status to check source errors, last successful cycles and copy progress. Monitor disk usage and the age of the latest expected measurement separately. Central alert delivery and ownership require site confirmation.
|-
| Application performance management || Measure copy-cycle duration, rows copied, lag relative to a changing reference tag, and response times for representative trends and scripts. Record the panel refresh interval with each result to distinguish collection lag from display lag.
|-
| Application development framework || Python and FastAPI backend, asyncpg for PostgreSQL, HTML and JavaScript frontend, uPlot trend charts and Grafana dashboards.
|-
| Data retention || The standard Windows installation has no configured age-based purge of local history. Initial backfill duration sets the starting point for collection. Site to define retention for local measurements, exports, logs and backups, who performs deletion, and the source archive horizon available for backfill.
|}

