== Restrictions ==
The reviewed package targets 64-bit Windows. It needs a writable installation directory and network access to the AVEVA Historian SQL endpoint. Charting continues from the local copy during a source interruption, subject to local service availability. Accuracy of per-item calculations depends on the source preserving the required events. The project source carries an MIT licence. Bundled components and site Historian access are subject to their respective terms. Production usage, distribution and any site-specific geographic restrictions require owner confirmation.

== Setup Instruction ==
# Follow the repository README section for the Windows package without Docker. Extract the approved offline build to a short, writable path with Latin characters. Record the deployed version and approved internal package location.
# Before network access is enabled, agree who can reach the management UI and Grafana, apply site access controls and replace the package's default credentials. Verify that the approved access path enforces the intended permissions.
# Start the package using start.bat. Open the local application and configure the AVEVA SQL Server endpoint, authentication method, include/exclude tag patterns, initial history window and poll interval.
# Run the connection test. Verify login, tag discovery and a sample history read. Start copying and compare a known tag's time, value and quality with the source.
# Open a trend and a numeric Stat panel in Grafana. For the weighing use case, reconcile results against a shift with known box counts and weights. Record the test interval and result.

== Backup and Recovery ==
Proposed procedure for the Windows package. The site backup schedule, destination, retention period, recovery owner and latest DR test result require confirmation.

# Back up the approved package version, its configuration (including .env), and the complete data directory. This includes PostgreSQL history, settings, scripts and copy progress (data\postgres), Grafana state (data\grafana) and dashboard files saved by the application (data\dashboards). Protect the backup as it can contain credentials.
# For a file-level backup, close the application, run stop.bat and verify that the packaged PostgreSQL and Grafana processes have stopped before copying the installation. Store a dated copy on approved storage outside the application host. Schedule the resulting downtime. If the site needs online backup, agree a database-consistent method and test it.
# Restore the matching package, configuration and consistent data copy to an isolated recovery host. Reapply directory permissions and network controls, start the package, then verify saved sources, scripts, Grafana dashboards and a reference trend.
# Once authorised to reconnect to Historian, check the saved copy position and reconcile the recovered interval against source records. Record backup timestamp, actual loss, recovery duration, outstanding gaps and the result of a reference dashboard check.

